   "fen": "rnbqk2r/ppp2ppp/5n2/3pP3/1bB5/2N5/PP3PPP/R1BQK1NR w KQkq d6 0 7",
   "line": [
    "d5"
   ],
   "spoken": "When calculating, always start with the simplest move — if it already leads to a good position, you save time and dodge mistakes. Here the straightforward captures win the queen, and the far-advanced passed pawn becomes a huge endgame asset: it ties down a rook, keeps the enemy king weak, and lets you castle long with check. A pawn like that is worth more than it looks.",
   "kind": "tactic"
  },
  {
   "ply": 13,
   "t": 218.5,
   "fen": "rnbqk2r/ppp2ppp/5P2/3p4/1bB5/2N5/PP3PPP/R1BQK1NR b KQkq - 0 7",
   "line": [
    "exf6"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 14,
   "t": 233.5,
   "fen": "rnb1k2r/ppp2ppp/5q2/3p4/1bB5/2N5/PP3PPP/R1BQK1NR w KQkq - 0 8",
   "line": [
    "Qxf6"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 15,
   "t": 278,
   "fen": "rnb1k2r/ppp2ppp/5q2/3B4/1b6/2N5/PP3PPP/R1BQK1NR b KQkq - 0 8",
   "line": [
    "Bxd5"
   ],
   "spoken": "The opponent's hope, that recapturing wins the piece back via a fork on the king and rook, fails — the fork is simply parried by interposing the bishop. So the central pawn can be safely collected, and it's just a clean piece to the good.",
   "kind": "tactic"
  },
  {
   "ply": 16,
   "t": 302.5,
   "fen": "rnb1k2r/ppp2ppp/5q2/3B4/8/2b5/PP3PPP/R1BQK1NR w KQkq - 0 9",
   "line": [
    "Bxc3+"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 17,
   "t": 303.5,
   "fen": "rnb1k2r/ppp2ppp/5q2/3B4/8/2P5/P4PPP/R1BQK1NR b KQkq - 0 9",
   "line": [
    "bxc3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 18,
   "t": 306,
   "fen": "rnb1k2r/ppp2ppp/8/3B4/8/2q5/P4PPP/R1BQK1NR w KQkq - 0 10",
   "line": [
    "Qxc3+"
   ],
   "spoken": "Here's the crucial follow-up to the interposition — with the e-file open, the queen steps to e2, pinning the enemy king to its queen and forcing the trade that wins. Whenever a file opens toward the kings, always look for a check on the second or seventh rank; it changes everything.",
   "kind": "tactic"
  },
  {
   "ply": 19,
   "t": 329,
   "fen": "rnb1k2r/ppp2ppp/8/3B4/8/2q5/P2B1PPP/R2QK1NR b KQkq - 1 10",
   "line": [
    "Bd2"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 21,
   "t": 339,
   "fen": "rnb1k2r/ppp2ppp/8/3Bq3/8/8/P2BQPPP/R3K1NR b KQkq - 3 11",
   "line": [
    "Qe5+",
    "Qe2"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 22,
   "t": 390.5,
   "fen": "r1b1k2r/pppn1ppp/8/3Bq3/8/8/P2BQPPP/R3K1NR w KQkq - 4 12",
   "line": [
    "Nd7"
   ],
   "spoken": "The enemy queen can't move until the king steps off the open file, so you dictate the terms — force the opponent to capture your queen, which lets your king come to the centre, exactly where it belongs in an endgame. Trading on your own terms turns the pin into an advantage.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 23,
   "t": 437,
   "fen": "r1b1k2r/pppn1ppp/8/3Bq3/8/5N2/P2BQPPP/R3K2R b KQkq - 5 12",
   "line": [
    "Nf3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 24,
   "t": 552,
   "fen": "r1b1k2r/pppnqppp/8/3B4/8/5N2/P2BQPPP/R3K2R w KQkq - 6 13",
